<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philippine Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a>–<a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Philippine Revolution was the armed struggle against Spanish rule launched by the Katipunan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret society</a> in August 1896. After a truce in 1897 it revived in 1898 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-aguinaldo">Emilio Aguinaldo</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a>, leading to the declaration of independence on 12 June 1898 and the First Philippine Republic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> then ceded the islands to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philippine-revolution.jpg" alt="Philippine Revolution" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Desconocido (pertenece a la misma sesión de fotos que File:Los defensores de Baler en Barcelona (septiembre de 1899, La … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:UltimosFilipinas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> had ruled the archipelago for 333 years. The colony was governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> from 1565 and paid for by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">galleon</a> trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a>, which ended in 1815; after Mexico became independent in 1821 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> was governed directly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>. Manila was opened to world trade by a royal decree of 1834, and revolutionary ideas from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> entered through books and fed the rise of an enlightened principalía class. Abuses by the Spanish government, army and clergy, exposed by the ilustrados, helped create a sense of common cause.</p><p>The Propaganda Movement of Filipinos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> published the newspaper La Solidaridad from 1889 to 1895 and pressed for reforms, but the colonial government did not carry out those the Spanish legislature passed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-rizal">José Rizal</a> founded La Liga Filipina in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, was arrested within days and was deported to Dapitan. Andrés Bonifacio and others founded the Katipunan on 7 July 1892; influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freemasonry">Freemasonry</a>, it advocated independence through armed revolt, and most of its members came from the lower and middle classes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Spanish authorities learned of the Katipunan in August 1896, and the revolution began prematurely. Bonifacio's attempt to besiege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> failed, but rebels in Cavite, led by Mariano Álvarez and the cousins Baldomero and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-aguinaldo">Emilio Aguinaldo</a>, won early victories. A rivalry grew between Bonifacio's Magdiwang faction and Aguinaldo's Magdalo. At the Tejeros elections of 1897 Aguinaldo was elected president, Bonifacio refused to recognise the result, and in May 1897 Bonifacio and his brother Procopio were executed after a trial for treason. Later that year Aguinaldo's government signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-biak-na-bato">Pact of Biak-na-Bato</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and revolutionary officers went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in April 1898, Commodore George Dewey's squadron defeated the Spanish fleet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manila-bay">Battle of Manila Bay</a> on 1 May. Aguinaldo returned on 19 May, and by June the rebels held nearly all the countryside while the cities stayed under Spanish control. On 12 June 1898 he issued the declaration of independence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kawit">Kawit</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> nor the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> recognised the declaration, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1898">Treaty of Paris</a>, which ended the war and Spanish rule, was signed without Filipino representatives. The Malolos Congress, whose delegates were all from the ilustrado class, approved a constitution on 21 January 1899. Fighting between Filipino and American forces broke out on 4 February 1899, beginning the Philippine-American War. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> became fully independent on 4 July 1946, fifty years after the revolution began.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philippine Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nationalism grew after the Cavite mutiny of 1872, and the execution of Jose Rizal in 1896 fed the <b>Philippine Revolution</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-the-philippines">Captaincy General of the Philippines</a></li><li><blockquote>The Katipunan, a secret society founded in Manila in 1892, launched the <b>Philippine Revolution</b> in August 1896.</blockquote><a href="/state/tagalog-republic">Tagalog Republic</a></li></ul></section>
